course: dictionary
lesson: 3886
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: French can mean three different things, and the words around it tell you which one. As a language, French is what people speak in France, Canada (Quebec), Belgium, Switzerland and many countries in Africa. «Our French lesson starts after break» means the school subject, because a lesson is something you learn in school.
explain: As an adjective, French means from France. In «a long French loaf» French tells you where the bread comes from. The French, with the in front, means the people of France, as in «The French are proud of their city of Paris».
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. Try «I speak from France» and it does not work, so French must mean the language in «I speak French».
fact: French is the language of France. It is also spoken in Canada (Quebec), Belgium, Switzerland and many countries in Africa.
fact: «In 1608 the French founded Québec and traded furs with First Nations by canoe across huge distances.»
fact: French has funny weather sayings: «un temps de chien» means "a dog's weather", a dreadful stormy day, and «il pleut des cordes» means "it's raining ropes", heavy rain.
fact: For how your body feels, French uses avoir (to have): «j'ai chaud» means I'm hot and «j'ai froid» means I'm cold.
fact: «Sensible» in French means sensitive, easily upset or touched. In English, sensible means sage or sensible, so it is a false friend.
exercise: «We bought a long French loaf at the market.» What does French describe here? | *Bread from France | A school subject | The language of France | The people of France
exercise: «The French built a city on the river in 1608.» Who are the French in this sentence? | A dog's weather | *The people of France | The language of Canada | A school subject
exercise: «Our French lesson starts after break.» What is French in this sentence? | *A school subject, the language | The people of Paris | A stormy day | Bread from France
test: Which city is the capital of France? | Quebec | *Paris | Geneva | Montreal
test: What does «il pleut des cordes» mean? | Wet and grey | I'm hot | A dog's weather | *It's raining ropes
exam: In «My French friend lives in Paris and speaks French.» which explanation is right? | Both words mean a dog's weather | *The first means from France, the second means the language | Both words mean the people of France | Both words mean I'm cold
